# Reach a RUNNING city, headlessly. Verified 2026-09-30.
#
# This is the route docs/RE_SCENARIO_NAV.md called impossible. It said: "steps
# 5-6 cannot [be scripted], because a headless run has no pointer for the shim
# to read, so it never pulses" - and that the clock therefore never advanced
# because the city was never created. Both halves of that turned out to be
# wrong: `mouseclick right` does reach the guest, and with it the city starts
# and sits on screen. The clock is still frozen anyway, which is a different
# bug. See docs/RE_CITY_FREEZE.md.
#
# The route, and why each step is shaped the way it is:
#
#   wait 250 / start / wait / down / wait / b      -> MAP SELECT
#   b x12, ~90 frames apart                         -> "Enter name of the city"
#
# The waits between the twelve `b` presses are load-bearing. The script parser
# accumulates `wait` into a pending counter and flushes it into the NEXT entry
# (host_main.c:781-852), so presses written back to back arrive on consecutive
# frames and the screen never advances. Twelve presses with no wait leaves you
# on MAP SELECT - measured, and it looks exactly like the presses not working.
#
#   press left                                      -> wraps the key list to its
#     The guest's key list is linear and `left` from the first key wraps to the
#     end. One press, and the hand goes to the right of row 1.
#
#   press down x4, then mouseclick right             -> ENT, then the city starts
#     The hand walks down the right-hand column and ENT is four rows below where
#     `left` leaves it. `right` is the button that selects the key under the
#     hand - `b` and `a` both fail here, which is why this took a sweep to find.
#
# The `down` presses must come BEFORE the click, not interleaved: the sweep that
# found this compared WRAM after each click and only the fourth produced a
# 70,284-byte change (54% of WRAM). Every other click moved 19-78 bytes, which
# is the idle noise floor.
#
# Soft-mouse caveat, if you extend this with `mousemove`: joypad_soft_mouse_map
# saturates its x accumulator at two thresholds, so a run of rightward moves
# leaves x permanently positive and the next `mousemove 0 <dy>` emits
# RIGHT|DOWN in one word, which the host reads as "both pressed" and cancels.
# Drain it with a `mousemove -4 0 2` first, or use `press` and skip the shim.
wait 250
press start 5
wait 120
press down 5
wait 120
press b 5
wait 120
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
press b 5
wait 90
wait 300
press left 5
wait 150
press down 5
wait 90
mouseclick right 6
wait 200
press down 5
wait 90
mouseclick right 6
wait 200
press down 5
wait 90
mouseclick right 6
wait 200
press down 5
wait 90
mouseclick right 6
wait 200
wait 400
